<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Addis Ababa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italo-ethiopian-war">First Italo-Ethiopian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> was signed in 1896 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the Ethiopian victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a>. It annulled the disputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a> and recognised Ethiopia's full independence. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> treat it as the diplomatic confirmation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>'s military triumph, which made Ethiopia one of the few African states to avoid European colonisation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The First Italo-Ethiopian War grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a> of 1889. Its Italian text obliged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to conduct all foreign affairs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in effect making it an Italian protectorate, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> version only gave Ethiopia the option of dealing with other powers through the Italian government. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> believed he had been deceived, and the Italians had meanwhile been encroaching on Ethiopian territory until fighting began in 1895.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> in March 1896, Menelik held 3,000 Italian prisoners and faced the demoralised remnants of the Italian army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, and news of the defeat forced Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi">Francesco Crispi</a> to resign. A secret agreement was negotiated days after the battle, and the final treaty superseded it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first Italian offer, presented by Major Tomasso Salsa on 11 March, was to abrogate Wuchale in exchange for a new treaty of peace and friendship, but it asked that Menelik accept the protection of no other power. According to the historian Harold G. Marcus, Menelik was so angered that he demanded the return of their secret truce and said he would hold Salsa hostage until then. Only on 23 August did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> agree to the unconditional abrogation of Wuchale and recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s sovereign independence, after which talks moved quickly to the signing on 26 October 1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The prisoners of war, whose captivity Marcus calls reasonably benign, were to be repatriated, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was to pay an indemnity of 10,000,000 lire for their upkeep. Italy kept most, if not all, of the land it had taken beyond the Mareb-Belessa and May/Muni rivers, and Ethiopian monarchists hold that Menelik gave away a sizable part of Tigray. The border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> was defined in agreements of 1900, 1902 and 1908. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> signed a treaty treating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as an equal in late January 1897 and the United Kingdom on 14 May 1897.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Addis Ababa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Addis Ababa</b> forced Italy to recognise Ethiopian independence, making Adwa the most significant African military victory of the colonial era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
